From 39f8651ca59db4761006cd7f2efb9872c49a8581 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pdale-boop <237306950+pdale-boop@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:51:47 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Sun shadows: Wind Waker HD's facing term and its light for
 the hour

What faces away from the sun is in shadow too, then HD's terrain curve 0.5 x (2s)^e, as HD's shadow
pre-pass and terrain shaders shade the scenery (off by default; DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_FACING=1, with
_FACING_MUL, _FACING_FAR_START/_END and DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_SHAPE to tune it). The facing term comes from
the scenery's smooth normals, made on the CPU while it is on (the GameCube's scenery has none: it is lit by
baked vertex colours), else from the depth's.

- Characters keep their own shading: the sea mask gains a green channel for skinned draws that write depth
  (Link's draws don't all cast), and the facing term leaves them and the sea out. The mask is drawn whenever
  the facing term needs it.
- aurora_set_sun_palette: HD's palette for the hour (linear): a shaded pixel is multiplied by HD's ambient
  (its shadow colour from above, BG0 C0 x its factor from the side, by 0.5 + 0.5 N.y) over its sunlight (BG0
  K0 x its factor), in the scene's gamma. Without a palette the fixed colour stays.
- The sun map's gap fill off (it found Link's own front and shaded most of him, which HD doesn't); the slope
  term near the camera as far off (20: at 10 Link's side turned from the sun shaded itself).
- Tests and debugging: DOL_AURORA_SUN_SHADOW_CHARACTER_CULL (character casters with one side culled), log
  lines for the facing term and per scenery format, debug views 9 (how lit), 10 (the facing term alone) and
  11 (the map alone).
